 A default send waits for the Host: Enter captures the draft text, its reference chips, and its attachments in the attempt, and the composer enters `sending`, with the editor and attachment rail read-only and Send showing progress, until the Host accepts the prompt, as its receipt or an Inbox row carrying its request id shows, whichever arrives first. Only then do the draft, its attachments, the occurrence table, and the undo history clear in one transaction, before the echo renders; a refusal or failure returns the composer to `plain` with the draft and attachments unchanged, so one message is in flight at a time. When a read-only input phase ends with focus on the document body, desktop returns focus to the editor and mobile moves it to the composer card without reopening the keyboard. A control the user focused during that phase keeps focus. `sendSession` registers a Session submission echo (`session.beginSubmission`) with the delivery mode before serializing, preserving selected image and file order; Session publishes it in `pendingSubmissions` once the Host accepts the prompt and derives the placement from that mode and its running state at registration, so idle sends use the transcript, busy Queue sends use QueueDock, and busy Steer sends use the pending-steering surface. It then yields one paint and cites the staged receipts of files and uploaded images; an image without an upload, as in a continuable subagent's draft, is encoded through the browser's native `FileReader` data-URL path. Command submissions use the same receipts for generic files, so sending `/goal` or `/plan` never reads those browser files again. The prompt reuses the submission `requestId`; Session retires the echo once at its display handoff, correlated by that `rpcId`. Command submissions keep the frozen `submitting` phase. An observed retirement immediately exposes each image preview through the durable cache, replaces it with the canonical URL after fetching the admitted attachment, revokes each URL after its use ends, and releases file cards; an accepted prompt whose echo retires unobserved releases its attachments, and Session scope disposal releases the attachments of a message the Host has not accepted. Selected generic files, and the images of an ordinary Session's draft, enter one FIFO background-upload queue as soon as they are added, and Send waits until each has a receipt; `maxConcurrentFileUploads` defaults to two active Worker transports, the Conversation service retains queued and active operations plus byte progress across Session navigation, and removing a draft skips its queued transfer or aborts its active transport. Where the browser shell exposes `__DSH_HOST_PATHS__` (the Desktop application), dropped or pasted folders and dropped, picked, or pasted non-image files with a real path become `@path` chips; images keep uploading. Drag-and-drop and paste identify directories through the browser entry API; when that API is absent or returns no entry, pasted items retain ordinary file handling. The file picker cannot select directories. References require the `ui-reference` plugin and the original paths to remain readable by the model's file tools. Paths inside the workspace are relative; others remain absolute. The whole batch is validated before insertion, retains source order and selected text, and uses whitespace-separated, closed-quote mentions. A browser without the bridge refuses dropped or pasted folders; a Desktop folder without a reported path is rejected separately. Continuable subagents accept pasted and dropped PNG, JPEG, WebP, and GIF images with the same projected limits and send them inline, because the Host stages uploads only for ordinary Sessions; a batch containing a generic file, unsupported image format, or directory is refused before intake. Their sends register the same local echo; the continuation request carries the echo's `requestId`, which the Host records as the child message's `rpcId`.
 
+Before a default send reaches its own Session, the composer offers it through the bail event `conversation/input-deliver`. A listener that returns a promise owns the delivery: it calls `delivery.send(session)` to hand the same draft, attachments included, to the Session it chooses, such as a new branch, and the composer settles with that outcome. A listener that returns `undefined` leaves the default delivery in place. Commands and the empty retry submission are not offered.
+
 Queued submission echoes show “Sending…” beside disabled edit, remove, and steer buttons; a collapsed dock keeps the sending status in its header. A matching Host queue row replaces the echo and enables each action according to its normal text-content and running-state requirements. Prompt acknowledgement alone does not enable queue actions. A refused or failed submission publishes no echo and leaves the draft in the composer with an error notice. A refusal from the Agent admission stage (`session/prompt-rejected`) shows its own text, unless `presented` says the refusing plugin explains it in its own interface, in which case the composer adds no notice.
 
 Disabled Send and Stop buttons suppress their tooltips, including a Stop button that becomes a disabled Send button when the turn ends. While a normal composer is running, its primary pointer action remains Stop when the draft is empty or input is unavailable. Actionable text or attachments switch the same seat to Send; clearing or successfully submitting the draft restores Stop. While a message waits for the Host, the seat shows its progress, and a separate Stop stays beside it. The busy-Enter setting selects the Queue or Steer delivery for ordinary Sessions and continuable children, and the running Send button delivers through the same mode plain Enter resolves to; while it is enabled (no upload pending) over a plain message draft its label names that mode (Queue message or Steer message), so the setting governs Enter and the button together while Cmd/Ctrl+Enter still uses the other mode, and idle sessions, empty drafts, and `/` command lines keep the plain Send label. Their QueueDock rows share Edit, Remove, and Steer, and an empty draft shares the steer-all chord. One-shot children remain read-only. Plan mode and active goals do not change attachment intake. Continuable children keep separate Send and Stop actions and accept pasted or dropped images, but expose no File row; creator availability does not disable input, and Host admission failures use the ordinary prompt error feedback ([decisions](../../../.agents/notes/archived/bug-fix/2026-08-20-running-draft-primary-send.md), [inbox controls](../../subagent/subagent/README.md)).
